course: dictionary
lesson: 7527
title: Praise and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Praise can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, to praise means to say that someone or something is good, and to admire them. As a noun, praise means words that show approval or admiration. «Parents often praise everything because they love you, even when the praise is not specific.» Here praise is a verb, and the word not specific gives you a clue: good praise says exactly what was good.
explain: Praise helps us learn. Teachers praise good work and effort, and praise for effort, such as «you worked hard», helps you keep trying. In stories, heroes are praised for brave acts. Phrases like «high praise», «words of praise» and «sing someone's praises» show that praise can be big, warm and admiring.
explain: Praise is not always honest, so check the context. A friend who says a new boy «has a nice personality» may be using praise in a sly way, to hint that he is not good-looking. Praise from a bot that has not seen your work tells you nothing, because bots tend to flatter. Ask yourself: does this praise say why, and does the person know what they are praising?
fact: Praise means to say that someone or something is good and that you admire them.
fact: As a noun, praise means words that show approval or admiration.
fact: Praise can also mean giving thanks and honour to God, as in religion.
fact: Being too keen to agree and praise is called being «sycophantic».
fact: Praise for effort, such as «you worked hard», helps you keep trying.
exercise: «Parents often praise everything because they love you, even when the praise is not specific.» What does not specific tell you about this praise? | It is always said in writing | *It does not say exactly what was good | It is always sung | It names every single thing you did
exercise: Teachers praise good work and effort. Which praise helps you keep trying? | Praise that says nothing about your work at all | *Praise for effort, such as «you worked hard» | A silent nod with no words | Praise that is given to everyone for everything
exercise: The teacher wrote «words of praise» on my page. What does «words of praise» mean? | Words that tell you the time | Words written in a foreign language | Words that are rude or unkind | *Words that show approval or admiration
test: Being too keen to agree and praise is called being what? | Glum | Curt | Treacherous | *Sycophantic
test: What does «sing someone's praises» most likely mean? | To sing a song badly | To ignore someone completely | *To speak very highly of someone | To argue loudly with someone
exam: Which praise is most useful to a learner? | Praise from a bot that has not seen your work | Praise that is given for everything, whatever you do | *Praise that says exactly what was good, like «you worked hard» | Praise that is said without looking at your work
